{% extends "admin/layout.twig" %}

{#
  THE ADMINISTRATOR'S HANDBOOK.

  ── WHO THIS IS FOR ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  Whoever is running the award, not whoever is changing the code. It says what each
  area is for, what the words mean, which buttons cannot be undone, and what to do
  when a screen says something alarming. It does not describe the database.

  ── WHY SO MUCH OF IT IS A LOOP ─────────────────────────────────────────────

  Every structural fact here is read from the code that implements it — the areas from
  `AdminNav::sections()`, the roles from `Permissions::MATRIX`, the weights and the
  quorum from `RuleEngine`, the verification words from `RegistryCheck::STATES`. See
  the controller for why: this repo's most expensive documented fault is prose
  outliving the rule it describes, and a handbook is that hazard with people told to
  trust it.

  So the rule is: if a fact lives somewhere in the code, it is looped in here. What is
  typed is judgement — why something is dangerous, what to do next — because that is
  the part no constant holds.

  Anything typed below that COULD have been computed is a bug waiting to happen.
#}

{% block topbar_title %}Handbook{% endblock %}

{% block head_styles %}
<style nonce="{{ csp_nonce }}">
  /* Reading measure first: this is the one admin page somebody reads rather than
     scans, and a 90ch line of body copy is why documentation goes unread. */
  .hb{ max-width:78ch; }
  .hb__lede{ margin:0 0 22px; font-size:14.5px; line-height:1.7; color:var(--ad-text,#10292C); }
  .hb h2{ margin:34px 0 0; padding-bottom:7px; border-bottom:1px solid var(--ad-line,#e5eae8);
    font-size:16px; font-weight:700; letter-spacing:-.01em; }
  .hb h3{ margin:22px 0 0; font-size:14px; font-weight:650; }
  .hb p{ margin:9px 0 0; font-size:13.5px; line-height:1.72; color:var(--ad-text,#10292C); }
  .hb ul, .hb ol{ margin:9px 0 0; padding-left:20px; }
  .hb li{ margin:5px 0; font-size:13.5px; line-height:1.65; }
  .hb code{ font-family:var(--ag-font-mono,monospace); font-size:12.5px;
    background:var(--ad-bg,#f4f6f5); padding:1px 5px; border-radius:4px; }

  /* A jump list, because thirteen sections is more than anybody scrolls. */
  .hb__toc{ margin:0 0 26px; padding:14px 16px; background:var(--ad-bg,#f4f6f5);
    border:1px solid var(--ad-line,#e5eae8); border-radius:12px; }
  .hb__toc ol{ margin:8px 0 0; padding-left:18px; columns:2; column-gap:26px; }
  .hb__toc li{ margin:3px 0; break-inside:avoid; }

  .hb__t{ width:100%; margin:12px 0 0; border-collapse:collapse; font-size:12.5px; }
  .hb__t th, .hb__t td{ padding:7px 9px; text-align:left; vertical-align:top;
    border-bottom:1px solid var(--ad-line,#e5eae8); }
  .hb__t th{ font-weight:650; background:var(--ad-bg,#f4f6f5); }
  .hb__t td:first-child{ white-space:nowrap; font-weight:600; }
  .hb__scroll{ overflow-x:auto; }

  /* Three weights of notice, and they are not decoration. `--stop` is for something
     that cannot be undone; `--warn` for something that surprises people; `--note` for
     a fact worth carrying. An operator learns the colours in one read. */
  .hb__n{ margin:14px 0 0; padding:11px 13px; border-radius:9px; border-left:3px solid;
    font-size:13px; line-height:1.65; }
  .hb__n--stop{ background:#fdecec; border-color:#b42318; }
  .hb__n--warn{ background:#fff8df; border-color:#d49a0e; }
  .hb__n--note{ background:#eef7ee; border-color:#237b22; }
  .hb__n b{ display:block; margin-bottom:2px; }

  .hb__you{ margin:0 0 22px; padding:12px 14px; border-radius:10px;
    background:var(--ad-ink,#10292C); color:#fff; font-size:13px; line-height:1.6; }
  .hb__you b{ color:#f3b416; }
</style>
{% endblock %}

{% block content %}
<div class="hb">

  <p class="hb__lede">This is the console for the Africa GATES awards, events, shop and
    donations. Everything below describes what an area is <em>for</em> and what happens
    when you press something in it. Where a figure or a permission appears, it is read
    from the running platform rather than typed here — so if this page and a screen ever
    disagree, the screen is right and this is a bug worth reporting.</p>

  {# What YOU can do, before anything about what exists. An administrator's first
     question is not "what is there" but "why can I not see it". #}
  {% if your_role %}
    <p class="hb__you">You are signed in as <b>{{ your_label }}</b>.
      {{ roles[your_role].blurb|default('') }}
      Areas your role cannot reach are not hidden to be unhelpful — they are not in your
      rail at all, so if somebody describes a screen you cannot find, check this against
      the permissions table below before assuming it is missing.</p>
  {% endif %}

  <nav class="hb__toc" aria-label="Contents">
    <strong style="font-size:12.5px">On this page</strong>
    <ol>
      <li><a href="#hb-nav">Getting around</a></li>
      <li><a href="#hb-areas">The areas</a></li>
      <li><a href="#hb-perms">Permissions</a></li>
      <li><a href="#hb-prog">Programmes and editions</a></li>
      <li><a href="#hb-score">How a score is worked out</a></li>
      <li><a href="#hb-release">Releasing a result</a></li>
      <li><a href="#hb-events">Events and the door</a></li>
      <li><a href="#hb-verify">Verification</a></li>
      <li><a href="#hb-find">Search and filtering</a></li>
      <li><a href="#hb-bulk">Bulk actions</a></li>
      <li><a href="#hb-single">Individual actions</a></li>
      <li><a href="#hb-trouble">When something looks wrong</a></li>
      <li><a href="#hb-never">Things to never do</a></li>
    </ol>
  </nav>

  {# ══ 1 ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ #}
  <h2 id="hb-nav">1 · Getting around</h2>
  <p>Three ways in, and they reach the same pages:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The rail</strong> on the left holds {{ areas|length }} headings. One opens
      at a time. Its items reappear as a row under the page title, so once you are inside
      an area you can move between its screens without going back to the rail.</li>
    <li><strong>Press <code>⌘K</code></strong> (or <code>Ctrl+K</code>) for the command
      palette and type any page name. This is the fastest route and the one worth
      learning — it reaches every page your role can open, in one keystroke.</li>
    <li><strong>The address bar.</strong> Every screen has a real URL you can bookmark and
      share with another administrator. Nothing here is behind a tab that only exists
      after a click.</li>
  </ul>
  <div class="hb__n hb__n--note"><b>The rail shows only what you may use.</b>
    Headings are gated by role, so two administrators can see different rails. That is
    working correctly.</div>

  {# ══ 2 ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ #}
  <h2 id="hb-areas">2 · The areas</h2>
  <p>Read from the rail itself, so this cannot drift from what you see on the left.</p>
  {% for a in areas %}
    <h3>{{ a.label }}</h3>
    <p>{{ a.tip }}
      {%- if a.gate %} Requires the <code>{{ a.gate }}</code> permission.
      {%- else %} Open to every role.{% endif %}</p>
    <ul>
      {% for i in a.items %}<li><a href="{{ i.href }}">{{ i.label }}</a></li>{% endfor %}
    </ul>
  {% endfor %}

  {# ══ 3 ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ #}
  <h2 id="hb-perms">3 · Permissions</h2>
  <p>Five roles. A role is set per administrator under <a href="/admin/admins">Admins</a>,
    and it decides which headings appear at all — not merely which buttons are greyed
    out.</p>
  <div class="hb__scroll">
    <table class="hb__t">
      <caption class="sr-only">What each role may reach</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Role</th><th scope="col">What it is for</th>
        <th scope="col">Areas it may reach</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {% for key, r in roles %}
          <tr>
            <td>{{ r.label }}</td>
            <td>{{ r.blurb }}</td>
            <td>{% for section, allowed in matrix %}{% if key in allowed %}<code>{{ section }}</code>{% if not loop.last %} {% endif %}{% endif %}{% endfor %}</td>
          </tr>
        {% endfor %}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="hb__n hb__n--warn"><b>Only a superadmin can change configuration.</b>
    That covers settings, other administrators, webhooks, the judging rubric and the judge
    list. It is deliberately the narrowest permission on the platform, because those
    screens can change how a result is calculated.</div>

  {# ══ 4 ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ #}
  <h2 id="hb-prog">4 · Programmes and editions</h2>
  <p>The shape is the same every year:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>A <strong>programme</strong> is the award itself and runs for years — one row that
      outlives every cycle inside it.</li>
    <li>An <strong>edition</strong> (a cycle) is one year of it. It carries the dates, and
      an edition label if you want one printed instead of the year.</li>
    <li><strong>Categories</strong> belong to an edition, never to the programme. A new
      edition starts with no categories, which is why last year's do not appear.</li>
    <li><strong>Nominees</strong> belong to a category.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>An edition moves through its phases <em>on its dates</em>, one step at a time:
    upcoming → nominations → shortlisting → voting → judging → results → archived. You set
    the dates on <a href="/admin/programmes">Awards &amp; cycles</a>; the platform moves
    the phase.</p>
  <div class="hb__n hb__n--warn"><b>A phase only ever moves forward.</b>
    You cannot set it back, and you cannot set an edition to <em>results</em> by hand —
    that is what the release action is for (§6). If a phase is wrong, fix the
    <em>dates</em>, not the status.</div>
  <div class="hb__n hb__n--note"><b>Every phase change is recorded.</b>
    Whether the platform made it or you did, with the date it was due and the date it was
    noticed. If an edition changed phase late, the gap is on the record rather than
    hidden.</div>

  {# ══ 5 ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ #}
  <h2 id="hb-score">5 · How a score is worked out</h2>
  <p>Out of 1,000, and read here from the settings actually in force:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Community vote —
      {{ (rules.community_weight * 1000)|round }} points.</strong>
      Split two ways. Seventy per cent of it is <em>how many separate verified people</em>
      backed the nominee; thirty per cent is their total votes. Both are measured against
      the largest vote total anybody reached <em>in the whole edition</em> — not in their
      own category.</li>
    <li><strong>Judging panel —
      {{ (rules.judge_weight * 1000)|round }} points.</strong>
      The panel's average mark out of ten, straight. No floor, no curve.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A nominee needs <strong>{{ rules.min_judges_per_nominee }} complete
    scorecard{{ rules.min_judges_per_nominee == 1 ? '' : 's' }}</strong> before they can win
    anything. Below that they still appear, marked as provisional, and their judging half
    counts as zero — understated on purpose rather than guessed at.</p>
  <div class="hb__n hb__n--note"><b>Why a leader can score well under 450.</b>
    Because both community components divide by the biggest <em>vote total</em>, a full
    community score means as many separate supporters as the biggest tally anybody managed.
    Two nominees on 2,000 votes each, where 2,000 is also the edition's largest tally:
    one backed by a thousand people scores 293, one backed by two scores 135. This is the
    question you will be asked most often.</div>
  <div class="hb__n hb__n--warn"><b>Changing a scoring setting changes published standings.</b>
    An edition whose results are announced keeps the numbers it was announced with only
    because they were sealed at release (§6). Change these for the <em>next</em> edition,
    not the one on the wall.</div>

  {# ══ 6 ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ #}
  <h2 id="hb-release">6 · Releasing a result</h2>
  <p>Go to <a href="/admin/result-release">Result release</a>, choose the edition, and read
    the table. It shows every scored nominee in the order the award is decided, computed
    now, with both halves of each score and who is excluded and why.</p>
  <p>When you are satisfied, press <strong>Release this edition</strong>. That does three
    things together: it moves the edition to results, promotes the winners, and
    <strong>seals</strong> the standing.</p>
  <div class="hb__n hb__n--note"><b>What sealing means, and why it matters.</b>
    The sealed figures are what the public pages publish from then on. Without it, a
    published page recalculates on every view — so a nominee's score would move whenever
    the panel finished a card or a setting changed. One real nominee went from 693 to 885
    across a week with nothing edited. Sealing is what makes an announced result an
    announced result.</div>
  <p>If an edition is already published but shows as not sealed, the same button reads
    <strong>Seal this standing as announced</strong>. That happens when an edition crossed
    its results date more than {{ grace_days }} days late: the platform corrected the
    phase but deliberately announced nothing, so there was nothing to seal. Sealing it is
    yours to do once the figures are right.</p>
  <div class="hb__n hb__n--warn"><b>Release tells people they won.</b>
    Promotion is what notifies a winner. Read the table first — the release screen exists
    so you never have to find out what it would do by doing it.</div>

  {# ══ 7 ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ #}
  <h2 id="hb-events">7 · Events and the door</h2>
  <p><a href="/admin/events">Events</a> holds each event, its ticket tiers, its registrations
    and its check-in. Tiers are ordered by the order you drag them into, not by price. A
    tier's colour comes from the event's own accent — change the accent and the whole
    ladder moves, including the dot on the printed ticket.</p>
  <p>On the night, the door screen scans tickets. It needs a camera and it works on a
    phone.</p>
  <div class="hb__n hb__n--warn"><b>Test the door on the actual device, in advance.</b>
    A blocked camera looks exactly like a broken scanner, and the fallback message tells
    the steward to type the code instead — so a device that cannot scan will get through
    the evening without anybody realising why it was slow. There is a manual code entry on
    the same screen; know where it is before the queue arrives.</div>

  {# ══ 8 ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ #}
  <h2 id="hb-verify">8 · Verification</h2>
  <p>Three different things get verified, and they are unrelated:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>A voter</strong> confirms their email or phone with a code before their vote
      counts toward the people half of a score.</li>
    <li><strong>A person claiming a nominee profile</strong> proves they are that nominee
      before they can edit it.</li>
    <li><strong>An organisation</strong> raising donations has its registration checked
      before it can receive money — CAC, and SCUML where it applies.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>On <a href="/admin/partner-orgs">Partner organisations</a>, each registration carries
    one of these states:</p>
  <div class="hb__scroll">
    <table class="hb__t">
      <caption class="sr-only">Registration check states</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">State</th><th scope="col">On screen</th>
        <th scope="col">What it means</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {% for key, label in check_states %}
          <tr><td><code>{{ key }}</code></td><td>{{ label }}</td>
              <td>{{ check_sense[key]|default('') }}</td></tr>
        {% endfor %}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>The public register is at <a href="{{ cac_search }}" target="_blank"
     rel="noopener noreferrer">search.cac.gov.ng</a>. If you confirm a registration by
    hand, your name goes on that confirmation.</p>
  <div class="hb__n hb__n--stop"><b>Rejected is a reason to stop.</b>
    It means a register or a person said the details do <em>not</em> match. It is not a
    formatting complaint, and approving an organisation over it means money settling into
    an account nobody has verified.</div>

  {# ══ 9 ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ #}
  <h2 id="hb-find">9 · Search and filtering</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong><code>⌘K</code></strong> finds a <em>page</em>. It does not search your
      data.</li>
    <li><strong>Settings has its own search box</strong> at the top. There are more
      settings than fit on a screen; type what you are looking for rather than scrolling
      for it. Pressing <code>/</code> puts the cursor in it.</li>
    <li><strong>Most lists filter through the URL.</strong> Which means a filtered list is
      a link — you can bookmark "nominations still awaiting review" and send it to
      somebody.</li>
    <li><strong><a href="/admin/data">All data</a></strong> is the last resort: a table
      browser across the platform's own records, filtered by what your role may see.
      Reach for a purpose-built screen first; it will explain what a column means and this
      will not.</li>
  </ul>

  {# ══ 10 ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ #}
  <h2 id="hb-bulk">10 · Bulk actions</h2>
  <p>Deliberately few. Each one exists because doing it one row at a time is how a stage
    gets skipped:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Questionnaires — send in bulk.</strong> The real case is "we have just
      approved sixty nominees". Opening sixty questionnaires individually is how ten never
      get opened.</li>
    <li><strong>Profiles and nominees — merge selected.</strong> Tick the rows that are the
      same person and merge them. There is an unmerge for both.</li>
    <li><strong>Integrity — mark flags reviewed.</strong> Tick the fraud flags you have
      looked at, so the queue reflects what is actually outstanding.</li>
  </ul>
  <div class="hb__n hb__n--warn"><b>Read the count above the button before you press it.</b>
    A bulk send sitting on a list of three hundred rows is one somebody presses by
    accident. The screens show you the number they are about to act on; that number is
    the check.</div>
  <div class="hb__n hb__n--note"><b>Nothing here deletes in bulk.</b>
    Anything that has been used — a criterion, a vote code, a released record — is retired
    rather than deleted, because receipts and published results point at it. If you want
    something gone from a screen, you are looking for retire, hide or unpublish.</div>

  {# ══ 11 ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ #}
  <h2 id="hb-single">11 · Individual actions</h2>
  <p>Two conventions worth knowing, because they are consistent everywhere:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>A destructive button asks first.</strong> If a confirmation did not appear,
      the action did not run.</li>
    <li><strong>Every action that changes a record is written to
      <a href="/admin/audit">the audit log</a></strong> with who did it and when — including
      the ones that were refused. A refused action is often the more interesting row.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Where a screen offers <em>retire</em> rather than <em>delete</em>, use it. Retiring
    stops something being offered in future without changing anything already published.</p>

  {# ══ 12 ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ #}
  <h2 id="hb-trouble">12 · When something looks wrong</h2>

  <h3>Email or SMS is not arriving</h3>
  <p>Open <a href="/admin/settings/providers">Integrations &amp; provider health</a> and
    press Run. It asks every provider a real question and reports what each one actually
    said. A provider that is misconfigured says so there; nothing else on the platform will
    tell you.</p>
  <div class="hb__n hb__n--warn"><b>A sandbox account looks like success.</b>
    Some gateways issue test credentials that authenticate, return a message id and deliver
    to a web simulator. Every screen reads as working and no phone rings. The Integrations
    page names which environment answered — read that word.</div>

  <h3>A nominee's score is not changing</h3>
  <p>If the edition has been released, that is correct: the published figures are the
    sealed ones and will not move. Check <a href="/admin/result-release">Result release</a>
    — it shows today's arithmetic beside the announced result and lists anywhere the two
    differ, which is the answer to a support call that starts "my score has changed".</p>

  <h3>A results date has passed and nothing happened</h3>
  <p>The date is a promise, not the release. Until an edition is actually released, the
    public page for it says the date was missed and that the award is not decided — it does
    not publish a standing. Release it from <a href="/admin/result-release">Result
    release</a> when the figures are right.</p>

  <h3>Scheduled work has not run</h3>
  <p>Reminders, sweeps and phase changes run on a schedule driven from outside the
    platform. If several things are late at once, that is the thing to check rather than
    each feature separately.</p>

  <h3>Something is on the public site that should not be</h3>
  <p>The sandbox creates <em>real</em> records with test flags, so that a rehearsal is a
    real rehearsal. Public pages exclude them. If test content is visible publicly, that is
    a bug worth reporting rather than something to clean up by hand.</p>

  {# ══ 13 ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ #}
  <h2 id="hb-never">13 · Things to never do</h2>
  <div class="hb__n hb__n--stop"><b>Do not change scoring settings on a released edition.</b>
    The sealed figures protect what was announced, but the release screen and every export
    will then disagree with the settings in force, and you will be explaining the
    difference for the rest of the programme. Change them for the next edition.</div>
  <div class="hb__n hb__n--stop"><b>Do not delete a category, criterion or code that has
    been used.</b> Ballots, receipts and published results point at them. Retire it.</div>
  <div class="hb__n hb__n--stop"><b>Do not approve an organisation whose registration check
    was rejected.</b> Ask them for corrected details and check again.</div>
  <div class="hb__n hb__n--stop"><b>Do not release an edition you have not read the table
    for.</b> It notifies the winners.</div>
  <div class="hb__n hb__n--note"><b>And one that is safe:</b> pressing <em>Run</em> on the
    Integrations page changes nothing. It only asks. Use it freely.</div>

  <h2>Something missing here?</h2>
  <p>If this handbook does not answer a question you actually had, that is worth saying —
    the gap is more useful than the guess. The
    <a href="/admin/assistant">AI assistant</a> can answer questions about the platform in
    conversation, and <a href="/admin/support">Support tickets</a> is where a member's own
    question arrives.</p>
</div>
{% endblock %}
